WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.529
Oh god, I’m back, I made it back and I’m
still alive.

00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:11.300
That… thing, the Old Man, it had been tormenting
me for months, following me, trapping me God

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.300
knows where.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.480
But I’m out now… and yet somehow that’s
just as bad.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.650
Let me explain…

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.630
It all really started about a hundred days
ago, I didn’t start keeping a tally of the

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.160
days until a little after things started to
get strange.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.500
But if I think back, the first sign happened
on a completely normal day.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.050
I had been stocking up on supplies with my
eldest, Jimmy.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.179
The superstore in town had a limit on how
much you could spend in a single transaction,

00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:38.999
so we had to hit up a few different places
for canned food to store in the bunker.

00:00:38.999 --> 00:00:43.050
When we got back, my wife Janey said she’d
noticed a stain upstairs.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.280
Sure enough, she pointed it out to me; a slightly
discolored patch on the ceiling above our

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.280
bed.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.819
There wasn’t much there at first, it just
looked like a little area of damp, but I promised

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.710
I’d have a look at it over the weekend.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:57.890
No point wasting money on a handyman, I didn’t
think much of it.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.069
I definitely didn’t think of it as anything
to worry about… but I was so, so wrong.

00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:08.050
The very next day, I was outside, making a
full inventory of everything in the bunker.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.440
We had everything, all kinds of practical
equipment for any manner of disaster.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.590
Not just food and water, but ropes and tools,
tents and sleeping bags in case my family

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.570
and I ever needed to abandon the bunker and
sleep in the wild.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:24.710
You see, my whole life I’d been sure that
something was coming, that out on the horizon,

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.110
the end of the world was waiting.

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:31.259
The way I saw it, if civilization was one
day going to completely collapse, then I needed

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:34.470
to prepare to make sure my family and I made
it through.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:38.880
Maybe part of it was the fear too, but when
you’re scared of something, you make yourself

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.359
ready for it, right?

00:01:40.359 --> 00:01:43.770
Anyway, it was while I was listing the food
rations and trying to figure out just how

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.219
many months we’d survive on them, when Janey
came in from the house.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:49.610
“Jake, that stain on the ceiling’s getting
bigger!”

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.060
she declared, dragging me back indoors to
show me.

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.351
As far as I could see, it hadn’t changed
much since the day before.

00:01:55.351 --> 00:01:59.479
The day after that though, that was a different
story.

00:01:59.479 --> 00:02:02.929
Janey didn’t have to point it out, I could
clearly tell that something wasn’t right

00:02:02.929 --> 00:02:04.759
about that mark on our ceiling.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:07.039
It wasn’t just bigger, it was leaking.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:11.520
But not just water, it didn’t seem like
it had come from a leaky pipe somewhere in

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.520
the house.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:17.250
There was a dark, gooey substance, almost
like tar, that looked as if it had bled through

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.240
the surface of the ceiling from the floor
above.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.410
Except, that was the weird thing: there was
no floor above, and our bedroom was on the

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:27.230
highest level of the house.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.660
Apart from the attic, of course.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.340
I climbed up on the bed to take a closer look
at the greasy patch that was now the size

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.980
of two dinner plates.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.840
Wearing a thick pair of gloves, I reached
up to wipe away some of the muck.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.580
Immediately, I could smell something burning;
the fingers of my glove had started to melt.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:45.690
I ran into the bathroom, and pushed it off
my hand from the wrist.

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:50.900
By now, this black ooze had started to spread
much further across the ceiling, reaching

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.580
to just above our bed.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.530
Janey told me she didn’t feel comfortable
sleeping under whatever that stuff was, after

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.160
it had eaten through my gloves.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.010
I told her she didn’t need to worry, it
wasn’t dripping from the ceiling, and we’d

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.650
be fine as long as we just didn’t touch
it.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.550
Even then, she still opted to sleep on the
couch.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.810
That night, I had a really weird dream.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:14.000
I was convinced that I’d woken up in the
middle of the night, restless and uneasy knowing

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.950
Janey wasn’t in the bed next to me.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:20.530
That’s when I looked up at that slick patch
of darkness smeared across the ceiling.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:26.700
But there was something else there too: a
face, a grotesque face staring down at me,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.880
grinning a wide, lipless smile.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.590
I’m sure it was a dream, it had to be, I’d
been putting off doing anything about the

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:35.230
stain, that’s why I dreamt about it, right?

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.500
Or maybe I was awake and it was just a trick
of the light…

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.790
“What do you mean you won’t call someone?”

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:41.790
Janey asked indignantly.

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.950
“I just don’t see the point!”

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:44.950
I argued.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.860
We had gotten into something of a heated debate
over what to do about the goop on our ceiling.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.120
I’d decided not to tell her about my nightmare,
but even then, she was determined that I get

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.670
a handyman or someone to come and take a look
at it.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.380
“It’d be a waste of money,” I tried
to convince her.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.911
“Why should we shell out money for someone
when I can just get some disinfectant and

00:04:01.911 --> 00:04:03.110
clean it up myself?”

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:07.080
“Because you don’t know what it is, even
if you could just wipe it off, it might come

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.460
back!” she yelled.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.390
“You’d rather try to sort it out quickly
so you can get back to working on your bunker!”

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.330
“That’s to keep us safe!”

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.330
I retorted.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.330
“Look, Jake.

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:20.549
I’ve humored it for long enough, but you
spend more time out there than looking after

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:22.470
your own house,” Janey sighed.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:28.090
I refused, I knew it was the stubborn, petty
thing to do, but I had taken Janey’s comments

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:29.430
about my bunker to heart.

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.670
She’d never minded before, always understood
that I was only doing it for us.

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:38.410
I think she saw the good place it came from
first, and that meant more than the actual

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:42.830
work I’d done stockpiling supplies and making
sure we’d have working plumbing after the

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:43.830
collapse.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.590
Later that day, I caught both Jimmy and Jake
Jr were lingering at the bedroom door, staring

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:50.210
up at the thick, dark sludge on the ceiling.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:53.940
The pair of them were both keeping themselves
behind the doorframe or gripping the door

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.350
itself like a shield.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:58.320
My boys were scared of it, it was clear as
day.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:04:59.930
“Dad, is the house sick?”

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.170
Jake Jr asked.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.170
“Houses don’t get sick,” his older brother
interjected.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.170
“It’s haunted…

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.480
there’s a monster in the attic, right Dad?”

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:12.300
“No monsters and the house isn’t sick,”
I replied, forcing a smile to my sons.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.950
“I’ll take care of it.”

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:17.970
About a week after the stain had first appeared,
it was time for the monthly drill.

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:21.280
Every four weeks, I’d gather my family and
we’d do a little practice just in case the

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.590
worst should happen.

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.090
At the sound of an air horn, we’d all have
to grab the bug out bags I’d prepared for

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:28.950
everyone, and run out to the bunker in under
five minutes.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:32.680
The boys were always eager, it helped that
they were both young enough that we could

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:33.720
treat it like a game.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.880
Janey, though, she didn’t want to get involved
in this drill.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.220
She stayed indoors, trying to play it off
to the kids as if she was just busy.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:45.550
But the side glance she shot at me said different,
and it was hard to avoid the guilt that came

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:46.550
with it.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.210
That, coupled with seeing the boy’s reaction
to it, convinced me that it would just be

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:53.981
easier to have someone come take a look at
the slimy patch above the bed.

00:05:53.981 --> 00:05:57.889
I’d needed to go to town the next day, another
supply run, so I picked up the number of a

00:05:57.889 --> 00:06:01.910
repair guy while I was out, even called him
on the way home, thinking that would make

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:03.010
it up to Janey.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:06.820
But as I pulled up to the driveway, I could
hear screaming coming from the house.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:10.910
I raced indoors and that’s when I saw my
wife, standing between our two boys and…

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:11.910
it.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:16.870
It was like a desiccated corpse, an Old Man
with viscous black grease leaking out of his

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.280
decaying body.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:23.530
The same substance that had pooled on our
bedroom ceiling… and was now covering Janey.

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:27.610
The Old Man had her arm gripped in his body
hand, the same fluid coating her skin and

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:28.610
burning her.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.890
She was shrieking in pain, tears running down
her face as the boys cowered behind her.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.260
I froze, I had no idea what to do… so I
ran.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:40.169
I had acted out of panic, and spent the next
week regretting it deeper than any mistake

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:41.169
I’d ever made.

00:06:41.169 --> 00:06:44.530
I’d left my bugout bag in the house and
just blindly sprinted straight for the bunker

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:47.020
while the screams of my wife and kids filled
the house.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.790
In the days that followed, everything fell
quiet, but even then, I barely slept.

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:55.060
Through a porthole in the heavy metal door,
I watched the rear of my home.

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.681
Occasionally I’d catch sight of movement
inside, some tiny bit of motion, usually at

00:06:59.681 --> 00:07:00.681
night.

00:07:00.681 --> 00:07:04.610
I kept hoping it would be Janey, or one of
the boys, having found a place to hide and

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.350
then making their way out the back door to
me.

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.730
But every time, it was only ever him.

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:15.260
The Old Man slunk through my family’s home,
staring out at me with lidless eyes, his jaw

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:18.470
locked in a twisted grin like he was enjoying
tormenting me.

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:22.250
A sharp knock on the bunker door woke me up
from the short, restless sleep.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.150
I sat bolt upright, immediately expecting
that thing to be on the other side.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:30.400
It was the handyman I’d called a few days
earlier – it seemed like such a trivial

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:31.400
matter now.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.820
“There was no answer at the front,” he
explained to me.

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.770
“Hope you don’t mind me coming around
the back, your side gate was open.”

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.180
Barely paying him any mind, I dashed towards
the house.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:44.070
There was no sign of the Old Man, but I couldn’t
find Janey’s body, or the two boys’ anywhere.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.350
I went to grab my bugout bag, only to run
into the handyman in the hallway.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.660
Before I could urge him to run, to leave the
house, the Old Man’s face appeared, pushing

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.970
through another slick, oily black puddle in
the wall, grabbing the other man and dragging

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.169
him in as I bolted for the door.

00:07:59.169 --> 00:08:02.780
Heading for the woods was my safest option
– no, my only option.

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:07.080
If I went to the police, there was no way
they’d believe such an outlandish story.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.870
They’d probably suspect me of murdering
my family, or think I was crazy if I told

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.650
them a zombie had attacked my home.

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:15.410
That got me thinking, what the hell was that
Old Man?

00:08:15.410 --> 00:08:19.800
He couldn’t have been a zombie, for a start
there was only one and he wasn’t turning

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:20.800
people.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.980
But there was something off about his actions,
almost like they weren’t mindless shuffling

00:08:24.980 --> 00:08:25.980
and attacks.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:27.310
There was an intent behind them.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:28.590
He was patient.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.879
Camping out in the woods brought me no joy,
even with all the supplies in my bag.

00:08:32.879 --> 00:08:36.849
Whenever I tried to sleep, I’d just lay
awake for hours, thinking of my family…

00:08:36.849 --> 00:08:39.209
and the grotesque face of the Old Man.

00:08:39.209 --> 00:08:44.199
Every sound in the night – the wind rustling
leaves, animals cracking sticks underfoot

00:08:44.199 --> 00:08:49.019
– in my mind, everything I heard was him
getting closer and closer.

00:08:49.019 --> 00:08:50.920
And pretty soon he did.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.529
I started keeping watch during the nights,
and I’d catch sight of a silhouetted figure

00:08:54.529 --> 00:08:55.839
in the darkness.

00:08:55.839 --> 00:08:57.559
Every few days, he would reappear.

00:08:57.559 --> 00:09:01.769
He’d never get close enough to attack me,
just enough to force me to pack up camp again

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:03.180
and relocate.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:06.890
Until one night, I could hear the sound of
gurgled breathing outside my tent.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:10.990
I reached for my flashlight, casting its light
over the walking corpse that stood outside.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:15.980
It recoiled from the bright light, but as
its decaying face illuminated, I ran, leaving

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.474
my campsite and bag where they were.

00:09:19.474 --> 00:09:22.079
I didn’t care, I just sprinted through the
woods, branches scratching at me like the

00:09:22.079 --> 00:09:25.279
decrepit bony fingers of the Old Man clawing
at my skin.

00:09:25.279 --> 00:09:29.089
I could hear water running, barely seeing
the stream in my flashlight before my foot

00:09:29.089 --> 00:09:30.250
sank into it.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:35.379
Wading through, I crawled out on the opposite
bank, cold, wet, and still trying to get away.

00:09:35.379 --> 00:09:39.459
The days that followed were hard, with no
supplies I had to survive on what little I

00:09:39.459 --> 00:09:40.470
had left in my pockets.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:45.480
My best bet, I thought, was to keep hiking,
keep putting distance between myself and him.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:49.629
And for a while, it was working… or I thought
it was.

00:09:49.629 --> 00:09:55.720
It wasn’t until I came across tracks, not
like animal tracks, not even an ordinary human’s

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:56.720
footprints.

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:01.180
They were thick with that black, burning tar,
melting into the forest ground in the shape

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.619
of the Old Man’s foot.

00:10:02.619 --> 00:10:04.269
I’d gone around in a circle.

00:10:04.269 --> 00:10:08.430
The whole time I thought I was getting away
from him, I had just been going around and

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:09.430
around.

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:13.519
When I stumbled upon what was still left of
my campsite, I should have been overjoyed,

00:10:13.519 --> 00:10:16.430
but instead, my heart dropped through to the
bottom of my stomach.

00:10:16.430 --> 00:10:20.329
I shouldn’t have stopped there, that was
what he wanted.

00:10:20.329 --> 00:10:23.800
But I was hungry, exhausted, and just needed
to sleep.

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:27.899
That was when he got me, I woke up to the
stench of some burning substance, only to

00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:32.609
find something locked tightly around my ankle;
one of the Old Man’s hands.

00:10:32.609 --> 00:10:37.679
Another of his greasy gateways had opened
on a nearby tree, and he was pulling me inside.

00:10:37.679 --> 00:10:42.279
The pain was what hit me first, before I could
even get a good look at where I was or what

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:43.360
was around me.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.519
Instead, all I felt was the most excruciating
agony I’d ever experienced.

00:10:48.519 --> 00:10:50.430
Looking down, I saw why.

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.730
The Old Man had two of his bony, clawed fingers
hooked into my ankle.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:59.660
Not just holding me by it, his fingers were
going through and it hurt.

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.269
The black sludge was still dripping from him
and onto me.

00:11:03.269 --> 00:11:07.350
The more of it passed through my clothes,
touched my skin, and mixed with my blood,

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.240
the more I felt it burning.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:13.380
Digging in the fingers of his other hand,
the Old Man pulled apart the bones in my lower

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:19.029
leg like the wishbone of a turkey, only with
a much louder and far more painful snap.

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:24.069
The shock left me unconscious again, lost
and now with half of my leg forcibly ripped

00:11:24.069 --> 00:11:25.069
away.

00:11:25.069 --> 00:11:29.810
I had no idea where I was, and for some strange
reason the Old Man had left me to wander around

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:30.810
freely.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.559
Although I’d soon find out why.

00:11:32.559 --> 00:11:38.170
It hurt just to move, but I wrapped my leg
up and crawled my way around the vast labyrinth.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:42.560
Even if I still had my bag, which had a compass
in it, it still would have been impossible

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:43.569
to navigate.

00:11:43.569 --> 00:11:48.420
I tried to take shelter under my jacket, hiding
and setting up as much of a camp as I could.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:52.920
Every time I thought I was safe, a greasy
black puddle would begin to form on the nearest

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:56.990
wall, and that same decaying face with the
lipless grin came through it.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:02.360
But that was why he let me wander, just so
he could make sure I never slept; my suffering

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:03.459
was a game to him.

00:12:03.459 --> 00:12:06.339
The longer I spent there, the more tired I
became.

00:12:06.339 --> 00:12:11.059
Through lack of sleep, searing pain of my
forcibly amputated leg, and the fear of trying

00:12:11.059 --> 00:12:14.389
to keep away from the Old Man, I started to
get low.

00:12:14.389 --> 00:12:16.850
I had no way of escaping.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:20.989
Even if I tried stepping through one of those
black puddles, it would just corrode me.

00:12:20.989 --> 00:12:24.470
But I started to think, maybe that would’ve
been better.

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:27.339
Getting home meant having to live without
Janey.

00:12:27.339 --> 00:12:31.540
She would still be gone, Jimmy and Jake Jr
weren’t going to be waiting for me if I

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:33.279
somehow managed to make it back.

00:12:33.279 --> 00:12:37.949
I had been keeping a tally of the days since
I’d left, adding on the week when the stain

00:12:37.949 --> 00:12:39.050
first appeared.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.670
It had been ninety days.

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:42.499
I couldn’t take anymore.

00:12:42.499 --> 00:12:44.920
I just wanted it over.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:49.259
I didn’t see the point to continuing this
torture, I wanted to leave and I wanted my

00:12:49.259 --> 00:12:50.319
family back.

00:12:50.319 --> 00:12:53.250
The only way to get both was to let the Old
Man get me.

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:57.600
But he must’ve known I was looking for him,
every time I yelled out into the confusing

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.680
maze I was stuck in, I never got an answer.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:02.360
He wouldn’t come and find me.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:07.199
I couldn’t tell if he knew how badly I was
suffering stuck here and just wanted to prolong

00:13:07.199 --> 00:13:09.279
that, or if he’d gotten bored of me.

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:13.249
Eventually, after over another week without
rest, I tried to sleep again.

00:13:13.249 --> 00:13:15.629
And that was when he came for me.

00:13:15.629 --> 00:13:20.110
Only I didn’t bother to run this time, as
I saw the corpse-like creature shambling towards

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:22.389
me, black grease covering its body.

00:13:22.389 --> 00:13:25.069
I closed my eyes and waited for it to happen.

00:13:25.069 --> 00:13:28.899
When I came back to consciousness, my vision
was filled with nothing but bright, white

00:13:28.899 --> 00:13:29.899
light.

00:13:29.899 --> 00:13:33.350
I thought that maybe this was it, that the
Old Man had caught me and decided to put an

00:13:33.350 --> 00:13:35.379
end to the chasing and torture.

00:13:35.379 --> 00:13:38.749
For a brief few seconds, I was certain that
I was dead.

00:13:38.749 --> 00:13:42.089
What else are you supposed to think when you
wake up squinting at a blinding light?

00:13:42.089 --> 00:13:46.339
But I wasn’t dead and hadn’t been delivered
to the afterlife.

00:13:46.339 --> 00:13:51.250
And I wasn’t still in that horrible labyrinth,
that nightmarish place I had just been for

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.410
over a month.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:56.679
Feeling the cold, hard ground, something in
the pit of my stomach told me I was back in

00:13:56.679 --> 00:13:58.149
the real world.

00:13:58.149 --> 00:14:02.149
Still unable to see properly, I raised a hand
to block out some of the glare, the sounds

00:14:02.149 --> 00:14:04.390
of heavy boots clomping towards me.

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:08.600
Two pairs of gloved hands reached out and
grabbed my arms, dragging me out of the path

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:10.129
of the intense light.

00:14:10.129 --> 00:14:14.029
As they did, I started blinking away the spots
in my vision, and that’s when I saw masses

00:14:14.029 --> 00:14:19.889
of machinery and the creature, the Old Man
caught in the beams of three white spotlights.

00:14:19.889 --> 00:14:24.600
I looked up at the people who had saved me,
a pair of figures in tactical gear, their

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.600
uniforms bearing the insignia of something
called the ‘SCP Foundation’.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:33.300
As grateful as I was to this Foundation for
pulling me out of that nightmare, and as glad

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:38.989
as I was to still be alive, I couldn’t help
but feel more and more uneasy as they debriefed

00:14:38.989 --> 00:14:39.989
me.

00:14:39.989 --> 00:14:45.790
Maybe it was just how interested they seemed
in my encounter with the Old Man – or SCP-106,

00:14:45.790 --> 00:14:49.750
as they called it – as I described what
had happened in a lengthy debrief.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.709
Or it might have been how quick they were
to shrug off what the Old Man had done to

00:14:53.709 --> 00:14:54.899
my family.

00:14:54.899 --> 00:14:59.309
Whatever it was, I didn’t trust them, even
though they seemed to believe my story.

00:14:59.309 --> 00:15:02.889
Recalling it all made me think of Janey and
my two boys again.

00:15:02.889 --> 00:15:07.879
I used to be so afraid of the world ending,
spent all that time and money making sure

00:15:07.879 --> 00:15:10.269
I was ready for when the end finally hit.

00:15:10.269 --> 00:15:15.649
But it had snuck up on me, it didn’t matter
that the Earth was still turning, my world

00:15:15.649 --> 00:15:16.649
was gone.

00:15:16.649 --> 00:15:20.399
And, I figured, that must have been why the
Old Man kept me alive.

00:15:20.399 --> 00:15:27.059
It could’ve easily killed me if it really
wanted to, but it let me live on, not just

00:15:27.059 --> 00:15:31.939
so it could torture me, but so I’d be alive
to know my family were gone.

00:15:31.939 --> 00:15:36.019
It wasn’t long after I realized this that
the Foundation offered me something, medicine

00:15:36.019 --> 00:15:38.049
they said would help me forget about it all.

00:15:38.049 --> 00:15:42.089
If I didn’t take it, I’d at least get
to remember what I used to have, but also

00:15:42.089 --> 00:15:45.029
live with the pain of not having it anymore.

00:15:45.029 --> 00:15:48.009
That was the Old Man’s real torture... so
I had to.

00:15:48.009 --> 00:15:53.319
As I washed down the pill with a swig of water,
I thought back to Janey and my sons with tears

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:57.309
in my eyes, wishing them goodbye before they
were really gone forever.

00:15:57.309 --> 00:16:03.730
Now go and check out “SCP-106 CONTAINMENT
BREACH” and “SCP-106 CAUSED A SCHOOL LOCKDOWN”

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:08.299
for further background information on this
sadistic and sinister anomaly, as well as

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:13.350
a few other situations unsuspecting victims
found themselves being hunted by the Old Man...

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:16.600
and whether or not they too lived to tell
about it.

